---
import AgentChecks from "../../components/AgentChecks.astro";
import { agents } from "../../data/agents";
import { skillCount } from "../../data/skills";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href } from "../../lib/site";

const nSkills = skillCount();
---

<Base
	title="Build with AI"
	description="Build Pebble3D games with Claude Code, Cursor, Codex or Copilot. Agent skills make your agent interview you, write a brief, build a complete game and verify it with screenshots before it reports done."
	nav="ai"
>
	<section class="wrap page-head">
		<p class="eyebrow">Build with AI</p>
		<h1>Your agent writes the scene. The repo tells it how, and proves it worked.</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			Pebble3D is written to be read by coding agents. The rules are in one file, every
			scene field is documented with its default, and every change can be checked with a
			command that writes an image and a report.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap" aria-label="Agents" style="padding-bottom: 24px">
		<a class="card skill-entry" href={href("build-with-ai/skills/")}>
			<span class="k">Making a game?</span>
			<span class="t sans">Ask in plain words. The agent interviews you first.</span>
			<span class="d">
				It asks at most six short questions, writes a brief for you to approve, then builds
				a complete game: title, play, results and restart, with game feel and one committed
				look. It verifies the game with screenshots and tests before it reports done.
			</span>
			<span class="go">The {nSkills} agent skills →</span>
		</a>
		<div class="grid agent-cards">
			{
				agents.map((a) => (
					<a class="card" href={href(`build-with-ai/${a.id}/`)}>
						<span class="t">{a.name}</span>
						<span class="d">{a.summary}</span>
						<span class="go">Setup and prompt →</span>
					</a>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="wrap prose">
		<h2>What the agent reads</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<a href={href("build-with-ai/skills/")}>Agent skills</a>: markdown playbooks in
				<code>skills/</code> (copied to <code>.claude/skills/</code>) for interviewing,
				scaffolding, verifying and polishing a game.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href={href("docs/agents/")}><code>AGENTS.md</code></a>: where things are, the
				commands, the invariants it must not break, and a before-you-finish checklist.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href={href("llms.txt")}><code>llms.txt</code></a> indexes every docs page, and
				<a href={href("llms-full.txt")}><code>llms-full.txt</code></a> is all of them in one
				file. Every docs page also has a raw markdown twin: add <code>.md</code> to its path,
				as in <a href={href("docs/getting-started.md")}><code>/docs/getting-started.md</code></a>.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href={href("scene.schema.json")}><code>scene.schema.json</code></a>: the JSON
				Schema for scene files, generated from the zod source, so a scene can be validated
				before it runs. The <a href={href("docs/scene-reference/")}>scene reference</a> lists
				every field.
			</li>
			<li>
				The <a href={href("docs/cookbook/")}>cookbook</a> and the
				<a href={href("docs/examples/")}>examples map</a> point at real code in the
				<a href={href("demos/")}>demos</a>, by file and line range.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>What the agent can verify</h2>
		<AgentChecks />

		<h2>Why this works for agents</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Scenes are data.</strong> A level is a JSON file with defaults for every
				field, not engine code, so a model edits a small, checkable surface.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Physics is deterministic.</strong> The same seed and inputs give the same
				state hash in Node and in Chromium, so "did my change break the sim?" has a yes or
				no answer. See <a href={href("docs/cookbook/deterministic-game-loop/")}>the deterministic game loop</a>.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Every game has a bot.</strong> <code>?demo=1</code> plays the game, so an
				agent can capture it mid-play without a person at the keyboard. See
				<a href={href("docs/cookbook/demo-bot-and-captures/")}>demo bots and captures</a>.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<p class="faint" style="font-size: 14px; margin-top: 3em">
			Product names belong to their owners. Pebble3D is not affiliated with or endorsed by
			any of them; these pages only describe how the repo's files work with each tool.
		</p>
	</article>
</Base>
